## 3. Mandatory Design Preservation Requirements

Preserve the existing:

- Tag provider names.
- Equipment folder hierarchy.
- UDT definition names.
- UDT member names.
- UDT inheritance relationships.
- UDT instance paths.
- Equipment identifiers.
- Tag data types.
- Alarm definitions and thresholds.
- Alarm notification configuration.
- Equipment status expressions.
- Equipment availability logic.
- Color and image configurations.
- Perspective and Vision bindings, as applicable.
- SCADA navigation.
- Historical data configuration.
- Security permissions.
- Existing scripts and calculation logic.

Do not replace, rename, relocate or recreate any existing UDT instance.

Do not modify parent UDT definitions during the initial controlled live test.

Do not deploy a new equipment hierarchy.

## 4. Phase A — FAT Baseline Capture

Before making changes:

1. Identify the currently deployed Ignition project and tag providers.
2. Export the existing UDT definitions.
3. Export the selected equipment instance and its complete effective configuration.
4. Capture relevant project resources and equipment screen bindings.
5. Identify inherited properties and existing instance-level overrides.
6. Capture current simulated tag values, quality and timestamps.
7. Capture current equipment status, alarms and visualization state.
8. Record historian settings and existing data destinations.
9. Record the gateway licensing or trial state.
10. Generate a timestamped baseline manifest with configuration hashes.

Use supported Ignition interfaces for configuration exports and changes.

Prepare and verify a targeted rollback procedure before implementing any live data source changes.

Do not rely solely on a complete gateway restore as the rollback mechanism.

## 5. Phase B — Existing UDT Architecture Review

Inspect the actual deployed UDT definitions and equipment instances.

Determine how the application currently implements:

- Simulated process measurements.
- Memory tags.
- Expression tags.
- OPC tags.
- Reference and derived tags.
- Equipment communication status.
- Equipment operating state.
- Alarm evaluation.
- Equipment image and color selection.
- Historical data collection.
- Simulation enable or disable mechanisms.

Trace simulated values back to their actual source.

Identify any gateway scripts, timer scripts, tag events, device simulators or other mechanisms that write simulated values into the equipment tags.

Do not allow a simulation writer to continue writing into a tag after transitioning that tag to a live OPC source.

Produce an Existing_UDT_Architecture_Report.md documenting the actual structure.

Compare the existing architecture against the following conceptual capabilities:

1. Process measurement source.
2. Equipment status and availability.
3. Alarm evaluation.
4. Visualization interface.
5. Equipment configuration.
6. Historical data acquisition.

The conceptual capabilities are not mandatory folder or tag names.

Do not recommend structural changes simply because the deployed hierarchy differs from this conceptual model.

## 6. Phase C — Determine Minimum Required Change

For each verified customer process parameter, identify:

- Existing full Ignition tag path.
- Parent UDT definition.
- UDT instance.
- Current effective value source.
- Current simulation source.
- Existing data type.
- Existing engineering units.
- Existing alarm configuration.
- Existing history configuration.
- Verified OPC connection.
- Verified OPC NodeId.
- Proposed instance-level property overrides.
- Required unit conversion, if any.
- Potential impact on status, alarm or graphical bindings.

Determine whether Ignition supports the required value source change through an instance-level override of the existing UDT member.

The preferred implementation is:

- Preserve the existing member tag path.
- Preserve the existing tag name.
- Override valueSource to OPC.
- Override opcServer to the verified connection.
- Override opcItemPath to the verified NodeId.
- Preserve all other effective properties.

If the existing application uses a separate simulation source or source-selection mechanism, prefer configuring that existing mechanism rather than introducing another one.

If instance-level overrides cannot support the required change, stop before modifying the UDT definition and provide a proposed alternative for review.

## 7. Phase D — Select Pilot Equipment

Select exactly one existing MFCS bioreactor equipment instance for the controlled test.

Use either U01 or U02, subject to confirmed physical equipment mapping.

Do not guess which SCADA equipment instance corresponds to a discovered OPC instrument.

Verify the association using available equipment inventory, serial number, endpoint, equipment identifiers and customer-approved mapping information.

If the physical equipment association cannot be established confidently, complete the discovery and proposed change plan but do not bind live values to an incorrectly identified SCADA asset.

Do not change any other MFCS or UNICORN equipment instances.

## 8. Phase E — Pre-Execution Safety and Impact Assessment

Before making the selected tags live:

- Verify that the OPC connection is configured for read-only acquisition.
- Identify all downstream alarm and status dependencies.
- Identify whether switching to live data may generate alarm notifications, emails or other external actions.
- Identify whether any existing scripts could write back to the equipment.
- Identify whether historian writes could be incorrectly attributed to a production equipment record.
- Confirm the approved commissioning window and affected equipment.

Do not perform equipment commands or OPC writes.

Do not disable or modify existing alarm definitions to conceal undesirable test results.

If the test may trigger unauthorized external notifications, production records or equipment commands, stop and document the required mitigation.

## 9. Phase F — Initial Live Data Test

Select two or three representative verified parameters from the selected equipment instance.

Preferred initial parameters:

- Temperature.
- pH.
- Dissolved oxygen.

Use the exact names already present in the existing UDT.

Verify that the selected parameters have:

- Confirmed OPC addresses.
- Valid Good quality.
- Compatible engineering units.
- Compatible data types.
- Confirmed physical equipment association.

Apply the minimum required instance-level data source changes.

Do not modify the parent UDT definition.

Perform the changes using a supported configuration mechanism that preserves inherited properties and existing overrides.

Immediately re-export the affected instance and verify the actual configuration differences.

If any unapproved property has changed, restore the baseline before continuing.

## 10. Phase G — End-to-End Verification

For each transitioned tag, verify the complete data path:

1. Actual equipment OPC measurement.
2. Ignition OPC connection.
3. Existing equipment UDT member.
4. Existing equipment process value display.
5. Existing SCADA equipment detail screen.
6. Existing overview or dashboard display, where applicable.
7. Existing alarm evaluation.
8. Existing equipment status logic.
9. Existing historian destination, where already configured.

Compare direct OPC values with the corresponding UDT values and displayed SCADA values.

Allow for normal polling latency, display rounding and documented engineering unit conversions.

Record:

- Direct OPC value.
- OPC quality.
- OPC timestamp.
- UDT value.
- UDT quality.
- UDT timestamp.
- Displayed SCADA value.
- Expected display precision.
- Alarm state.
- Equipment status.
- Screenshot reference.

Observe the data over a bounded monitoring period.

Confirm that live values originate from OPC rather than the previous simulation mechanism.

Do not initiate equipment operation to generate changing process measurements.

## 11. Phase H — SCADA Regression Assessment

Using the existing FAT-approved screens, verify:

- Equipment remains in its original location.
- Equipment navigation remains functional.
- Equipment graphics remain unchanged.
- Images render correctly.
- Status colors reflect the existing status logic.
- Process values display correctly.
- Units remain correct.
- Alarm indicators remain functional.
- No broken tag bindings appear.
- No new expression errors appear.
- Other simulated equipment remains unaffected.

Distinguish expected changes caused by live process values from unintended configuration changes.

For example, an existing equipment color changing in response to a genuine status change is acceptable. Modification of the underlying color-selection logic is not.

Do not deliberately force a process alarm or alter an alarm threshold to demonstrate alarm functionality.

Capture relevant before-and-after screenshots.

## 12. Phase I — Expand Pilot to Remaining Verified Parameters

If the initial live test succeeds, evaluate the remaining verified parameters for the same equipment instance.

Extend the live mapping only to parameters that meet all of the following:

- Physical equipment association confirmed.
- Exact OPC address verified.
- Valid OPC data quality.
- Parameter semantics confirmed.
- Engineering units compatible.
- Existing tag data type compatible.
- No unauthorized downstream effects.

Retain unresolved or ambiguous parameters in their existing configuration.

Specifically review MFCS CO2SP engineering units and determine whether the intended customer parameter is a setpoint or controller value.

Do not implement undocumented unit conversions or substitute measurements without approval.

Do not extend the test to a second equipment instance.

## 13. Phase J — UNICORN Assessment Only

Inspect the existing deployed chromatography UDT and compare it against the discovered UNICORN candidate NodeIds.

Preserve its existing configuration.

Identify how the existing tags could be transitioned from simulation to OPC when valid process data becomes available.

Identify username-dependent addressing and any initialization requirements.

Do not call ConnectSystem.

Do not write ConnectSystemTag.

Do not change AssignState.

Do not convert existing simulated chromatography process tags to live OPC sources that currently return Good_NoData.

Prepare a separate targeted UNICORN integration change plan for execution after View-mode data acquisition has been verified.

## 14. Phase K — Configuration Difference Verification

Compare the pre-test and post-test configurations.

Verify that:

- Parent UDT definitions are unchanged.
- Equipment hierarchy is unchanged.
- SCADA project resources are unchanged.
- Alarm definitions are unchanged.
- Status expressions are unchanged.
- Visualization properties are unchanged.
- Other equipment instances are unchanged.
- Only approved data source properties have changed on the selected equipment instance.

Generate both human-readable and machine-readable configuration differences.

Any unapproved structural or functional configuration change shall be considered a commissioning failure and shall trigger rollback.

## 15. Phase L — Rollback

Prepare a targeted rollback package that restores:

- Original tag value sources.
- Original OPC connection references, if applicable.
- Original OPC item paths, if applicable.
- Original simulation behavior.
- Original instance override configuration.

Preserve the distinction between inherited properties and explicitly overridden properties.

Do not restore an inherited property by unnecessarily creating a permanent override.

Do not restart the entire gateway or restore the complete gateway backup unless separately approved.

If the pilot succeeds, leave the verified pilot equipment connected to live OPC data for Acropolis review, provided the approved commissioning window permits it.

If the test fails, data quality deteriorates, unauthorized effects occur or the configuration differs from the approved change set, restore the original simulation configuration.

Document the final disposition.

